//! The farm atlas set: a Stardew-Valley-shaped farming game's art, in five sheets.
//!
//! The rest of the generator draws one world — a town. This module draws a
//! *farm*: ground you hoe and water, six crops with five growth stages each,
//! the props and buildings a farm is furnished with, two villagers and a UI
//! kit. It is deliberately self-contained: it shares [`crate::draw`] and the
//! engine's 24-colour palette, but nothing here is read by the town assets, and
//! nothing in the town assets is read by it.
//!
//! ## Why it is separate
//!
//! The farm is generated by `noxel-gen farm --out DIR`, which writes five atlases
//! into `DIR/farm/` plus a contact sheet. Each atlas is an
//! [`noxel_asset::atlas::Atlas`] serialised with [`Atlas::to_json`] next to the
//! PNG it describes, so a game loads `terrain.png` with `Atlas::from_json` and
//! looks a region up by name. The names are the contract: they are listed in the
//! `const` arrays below and the tests assert them literally, so renaming a
//! sprite is a build failure rather than a silent break in a game's art.
//!
//! ## The pixel-art rules this module holds to
//!
//! * **Hard edges.** Every shape is an integer test (a disc is
//!   `dx² + dy² <= r²`, a rounded corner is a quarter circle), so no pixel is
//!   ever half-covered. Alpha is either 0 or 255 in every sprite, and a test
//!   proves it.
//! * **Drawn at final size.** Nothing is an upscaled smaller sprite; the only
//!   scaling anywhere is [`crate::farm_preview`], which blows the finished art
//!   up for a human to look at.
//! * **One light.** The sun is up and to the left in every sprite: the top and
//!   left of a volume catch the highlight ramp and the bottom and right fall
//!   into the shade ramp. The 16x16 ground tiles are the exception that proves
//!   it — a tile whose top edge is lit and whose bottom edge is dark *is* a
//!   visible grid line, so the ground carries its lighting inside the pattern
//!   instead of on its border.
//! * **One outline.** Every free-standing sprite is finished with
//!   [`draw::outline`] in [`SHADOW`], so a rock and a barn read the same way
//!   against the ground.
//! * **Palette only.** [`PALETTE`] is the complete list of colours this module
//!   may write, and [`write`] refuses to emit a sheet that contains anything
//!   else.
//!
//! ## Seams
//!
//! Ground tiles are meant to be laid in runs, so all fourteen of them are
//! seamless, not just `grass`. Every one is built from functions that are
//! periodic with period [`TILE`]: the value noise is sampled so that sixteen
//! pixels is a whole number of lattice cells (see [`terrain::water_pixel`] for
//! the same trick), coordinate hashes are taken on the tile's own coordinates,
//! and no routine treats the tile's edge specially. A field of any of them
//! repeats without a grid line.
//!
//! [`terrain::water_pixel`]: crate::terrain

